ose another candidate as stronger overall, maybe Candidate B, who is very conscientious, handles stress well, good at weather assessment, but also makes nasty remarks about colleagues (crew cooperation issue). Candidate A has quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, but communication difficulty and egocentric. Candidate D has responsibility, problem solving, but hot temper and know-it-all.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. We could either stay with Candidate C but note concerns, or shift to another candidate. Let's evaluate: Candidate C's strengths are strong situational awareness, leadership, technical. Weaknesses: show-off, not open to new ideas, unorganized. Those are significant for crew cooperation and adaptability. Candidate B is conscientious, handles stress, good weather assessment, but makes nasty remarks (communication). Candidate A is quick decisions, calm, but egocentric, poor communication, not willing to further education. Candidate D is responsible, problem solving, but hot temper, know-it-all, arrogance.\n\nWhich candidate best overall? Hard. Perhaps Candidate B's strengths in reliability and stress resilience are crucial, and his communication issue is nasty remarks, which is serious. Candidate C's show-off and not open to new ideas could also be serious. Which is worse? Both affect crew cooperation. But Candidate C also has strong leadership, which may mitigate. However, being not open to new ideas is a big issue for adaptability. Candidate B's nasty remarks are also serious for professional communication. Might be similar.\n\nWe could propose that we need more info on crew cooperation for C, but we have facts: show-off, not open to new ideas. So we can argue that these concerns may outweigh strengths, and perhaps Candidate B is a better fit due to conscientiousness and stress handling, despite his nasty remarks, but we need to weigh.\n\nWe could also ask for more info from other agents about crew cooperation for C or B. But we have some facts. We can